# Net-Razor configuration. Copy to ~/.net-razor/.env and fill in what you need.
#   mkdir -p ~/.net-razor && cp .env.example ~/.net-razor/.env
#
# Credentials and operator data live in ~/.net-razor, not the checkout. An MCP
# host picks the working directory, so anything resolved from the checkout is
# found only when something happens to launch from the right place -- and it puts
# secrets in a directory under version control.
#
# The source lists are NOT here. They live in their own files:
#   cp channels.example.txt ~/.net-razor/channels.txt      # YouTube channels
#   $EDITOR ~/.net-razor/podcasts.txt                      # podcast RSS feeds
#
# Note: a variable already set in your shell overrides the value here, silently.

# --- Storage / runtime (optional) ---
# A relative path here resolves against ~/.net-razor, not the checkout.
# DATABASE_PATH=data/net_razor_audit.db
# CHANNELS_FILE=channels.txt
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO
# Logs go to stderr by default, which an MCP host often discards. Set LOG_FILE to
# also write them to a file you can tail.
# LOG_FILE=logs/net-razor.log
# REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# --- Podcasts ---
# Feeds live in ~/.net-razor/podcasts.txt, one canonical RSS URL per line.
# PODCASTS_FILE=podcasts.txt
# PODCAST_MAX_TRANSCRIPT_CHARS=12000
#
# Local Whisper transcription for episodes whose show publishes no transcript.
# Off by default. Turning it on needs: pip install -e '.[whisper]' (Apple Silicon
# only), ffmpeg on PATH, and ~1.5 GB of model downloaded on first use. It runs in
# a subprocess that exits, using about 4 GiB while it works. Expect roughly one
# minute per twenty minutes of audio.
# PODCAST_WHISPER_ENABLED=false
# PODCAST_WHISPER_MODEL=mlx-community/whisper-large-v3-turbo
# Ceiling before the subprocess is killed. A three-hour episode measures at ~8
# minutes, so this leaves plenty of room.
# PODCAST_WHISPER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=900
# PODCAST_MAX_AUDIO_BYTES=524288000
# PODCAST_AUDIO_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300

# --- X (required for X search) ---
# Session cookies from a logged-in x.com session. These authenticate as a real
# account: the risk of heavy use is suspension of that account, not a bill.
AUTH_TOKEN=
CT0=
# X search shells out to Node, located via the launching process's PATH. If your
# MCP host launches with a sparse PATH, set an absolute path so node is always found:
# NODE_BINARY=/opt/homebrew/bin/node

# --- YouTube ---
# YOUTUBE_API_KEY is required only for yt_search (query search). The channel digest,
# new-videos queue and transcript fetch are key-free (public RSS + transcript endpoints).
YOUTUBE_API_KEY=
# yt_search only: 'broad' (all of YouTube) or 'channels' (restrict to channels.txt).
# A typo is rejected at startup rather than silently treated as 'broad'.
YT_SEARCH_MODE=broad
# Proxy for the unauthenticated RSS + transcript fetches (a residential proxy avoids
# IP blocks). The Data API is identified by its key, so it is left un-proxied.
# YT_PROXY_URL=
# Default for the channel digest's cross-run dedup (skip videos seen in prior runs).
# Set true for a scheduled daily digest; a call's own only_new argument overrides it.
# YT_DIGEST_ONLY_NEW=false
# Default for skipping videos with no fetchable transcript (e.g. captions disabled)
# instead of returning the description as a fallback. A call's own argument overrides it.
# YT_DIGEST_REQUIRE_TRANSCRIPT=false
# Characters of transcript returned per part (0 = the whole transcript in one response).
# ~40000 (~10k tokens) covers a ~35-minute video; longer ones are returned in parts.
# YT_MAX_TRANSCRIPT_CHARS=40000
